1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,740
Physics Universe. Episode One. Chemical reactions and equations. Right now, half the things around you are quietly destroying their own identity, and you never notice. Let's catch them in the act.

2
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:25,150
Two clear liquids on the bench. Lead nitrate, and potassium iodide. Both look completely innocent.

3
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:28,560
Now add a single drop.

4
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:33,850
Swirl. And something solid appears out of nowhere.

5
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:41,020
A bright yellow precipitate. Two clear liquids just made a brand new substance.

6
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:48,990
Experiment two. Zinc granules, and dilute acid. Corrosive, so handle with care.

7
00:00:48,990 --> 00:00:51,800
Pour the acid in.

8
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:57,570
Fizzz! Bubbles. That's hydrogen gas escaping.

9
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:05,780
And now the flask is hot. Gas evolved, temperature rises. Two clues in one reaction.

10
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:14,990
So how do you know a reaction really happened? Four clues. Clue one, change in state. A precipitate forms.

11
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:22,940
Clue two, change in colour. One drop, and the whole flask changes identity.

12
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:28,990
Clue three, evolution of a gas. Something new is leaving.

13
00:01:28,990 --> 00:01:38,840
Clue four, change in temperature. State, colour, gas, temperature. That is your checklist for every reaction.

14
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:47,790
Knowledge check. Question one. What white powder forms when magnesium burns in air? Magnesium oxide.

15
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:57,640
Question two. Which two solutions are mixed in activity one point two? Lead nitrate, and potassium iodide.

16
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:11,890
Question three. Name all four observations that identify a chemical reaction. Change in state. Change in colour. Evolution of a gas. Change in temperature. Full marks.

17
00:02:11,890 --> 00:02:19,490
That's episode one. Watch the clues, they never lie. Physics Universe.

